## Why this assignment exists

The expensive part of a design document is the thinking, and most of that happens at the whiteboard: deciding what the boxes are, labeling the arrows, and arguing about which alternative to take. Writing the draft is mostly transcription. Doing it while the boards are fresh keeps the decisions from being forgotten or quietly rewritten.

Engineering teams work this way almost everywhere. A design doc goes out for comment while it can still change, and the review shapes the version people build from. The doc that gets the most useful review is the one sent out whole and rough: a reviewer can question a component diagram they can see, but not one that is still in somebody's head.

*In this course,* the <course-link type="page" id="pg-rubrics">CP5 rubric</course-link> scores **Readability & revision**, and part of that is visible improvement from draft to final. This upload is the draft that improvement is measured against.

---

## What to produce

A draft in the template's structure. At minimum it covers:

1. **§3 System-context diagram**: a photo of the board is fine.
2. **§4 Architecture and components**: the component diagram, plus the core flow traced end to end and the failure case.
3. **§5 Data model**: what state exists and which component owns it.
4. **§6 Technology choices**: the **two argued decisions** from the workshop, each with a real alternative and the reason you chose against it.

Skeleton prose under each section is enough. The other sections can stay as headings with a line or two, or with a note on what is still open.

